<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="1,4-dihydroxy-2-naphthoate biosynthesis II (plants)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5791" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>|FRAME: DIHYDROXYNAPHTHOATE| is a branch point metabolite leading to the biosynthesis of menaquinone (vitamin K2, in bacteria), |FRAME: 2-METHYL-3-PHYTYL-14-NAPHTHOQUINONE| (vitamin K1 in plants), and many plant pigments, including some two-ring naphthoquinones (such as |FRAME: CPD-9608| and |FRAME: HYDROXYNAPHTHOQUINONE|) and the three-ring anthraquinones (such as |FRAME: ALIZARIN| and |FRAME: CPD-9759|). These pigments are mostly found in |FRAME: TAX-24966|, where at least some of them are phytoalexins involved in plant defense. Many of these pigments were used as dyes since the early human history. The benzenoid ring (or A ring) of 1,4-dihydroxy-2-naphthoate is derived from |FRAME: CHORISMATE|, derived via the shikimate pathway. The quinone ring (or B ring) is formed by addition of |FRAME: 2-KETOGLUTARATE|. In plant cell cultures protein synthesis and enzyme activities of |FRAME: MONOMER-13723|, |FRAME: MONOMER-11678|, and |FRAME: MONOMER-11669| were found coincide with quinone production |CITS: [Tegelen99][Sieweke92]|. Note that the plant pathway and the bacterial pathway differ at the step of |FRAME: MONOMER-11669|. The plant enzyme, shown here, hydrolyzes ATP to ADP and phosphate. The bacterial enzymes, such as |FRAME: MENE-CPLX| from |FRAME: TAX-83333|, hydrolyze ATP to AMP and pyrophosphate.</Description>
