    <DataNode elementId="AT1G68890-MONOMER" textLabel="PHYLLO [multifunctional]" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q15KI9" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="335" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">PHYLLO is a multifunctional enzyme that appears to be derived from the fusion of 4 eubacterial genes, &lt;i&gt;menF&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;menH&lt;/i&gt;, &lt;i&gt;menD&lt;/i&gt;, and &lt;i&gt;menC&lt;/i&gt;. Based on analyses of several mutants in this gene, PHYLLO clearly plays a role in |FRAME: PWY-5863 phylloquinol (vitamin K1) biosynthesis| upstream of |FRAME: AT1G60600-MONOMER ABC4|, but its exact enzymatic activities have not yet been experimentally confirmed |CITS: [16617180]|. The portion of the protein corresponding to the bacterial menF enzyme is lacking part of the expected C-terminal portion of the enzyme, and the |FRAME: ISOCHORSYN-RXN 5.4.4.2/ isochorismate synthase activity| associated with this enzyme in bacteria appears to be carried out, instead by the |FRAME: AT1G74710-MONOMER ICS1| and ICS2 enzymes of Arabidopsis |CITS: [16617180]|. The menD (|FRAME:2.5.1.64-RXN 2.2.1.9 / 2-succinyl-5-enolpyruvyl-6-hydroxy-3-cyclohexene-1-carboxylic-acid synthase|) and menC |FRAME:O-SUCCINYLBENZOATE-COA-SYN-RXN in KB METABASE 4.2.1.113 / &lt;i&gt;o&lt;/i&gt;-succinylbenzoic acid synthase|) activities are expected to be intact and to enable PHYLLO to perform both of these reactions |CITS: [16617180]|. The enzymatic function of the menH portion of the enzyme remains most unclear, in part, due to changing activities attributed to the &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; protein over time. At the time when PHYLLO was initially characterized, menH was originally believed to have activity late in the pathway as a |FRAME: RXN-9311 DHNA-CoA thioesterase| |CITS: [11153266]|, however, later analyses revealed that the &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; purified enzyme is not a functional thiolase, but actually carries out the earlier |FRAME: RXN-9310 4.2.99.20| reaction |CITS: [18284213][19545176]|. However, to further complicate matters, there is one report that purified menH from &lt;i&gt;E.coli&lt;/i&gt; also has palmityl-CoA esterase activity |CITS: [15808744]|. None of these activities have been directly tests in PHYLLO. Nevertheless, there is evidence that Arabidopsis has two other enzymes (|FRAME: AT1G48320-MONOMER DHNAT1| and |FRAME: AT5G48950-MONOMER DHNAT2|) which do act as functional DHNA-CoA thioesterases. Morevoer, this activity appears to localize to the peroxisomes whereas a fluorescently-tagged PHYLLO protein is found in the chloroplast |CITS: [22372525][16617180]|. This suggests that PHYLLO is not catalzying |FRAME: RXN-9311 3.1.2.28| &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt;, but no enzymatic activity has been experimentally associated with the menH C-terminal portion of the protein to date |CITS: [16617180]|.</Comment>
