    <DataNode elementId="AT5G48950-MONOMER" textLabel="1,4-dihydroxy-2-naphthoyl-CoA thioesterase 2" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FI76" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, dreher">DHNAT2 has DHNA-CoA thioesterase activity based on &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays using recombinant enzymes, as well as by its ability to complement a Synechocystis strain &amp;Delta;slr0204 that lacks this activity. In addition, a knockdown &lt;i&gt;dhnat-2&lt;/i&gt; mutant has reduced DHNA-CoA thioesterase activity as well as lower levels of phylloquinone |CITS: 22372525|. This enzyme has a notable preference for DHNA-CoA in the &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays. It shows some activity against |FRAME:BENZOYLCOA benzoyl-CoA|, but it is an order of magnitude lower than its activity against |FRAME: CPD-9925 DHNA-CoA|. Recombinant DHNAT2 also has no, or only trace activity when tested against, |FRAME:CPD-207 phenylacetyl-CoA|, |FRAME:SUC-COA succinyl-CoA|, and |FRAME:PALMITYL-COA palmitoyl-CoA||CITS: 22372525|. DHNAT2 has a peroxisomal targeting signal and a DHNAT2:GFP fusion protein appears to be a peroxisomal enzyme &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [22372525][ 19329564][ 15333753][ 15208424]|. Interestingly, the experimentally characterized DHNA-CoA thioesterase |FRAME:MONOMER-15406 Slr0204| found in the cyanobacterial species |FRAME:TAX-1148 &lt;i&gt;Synechocystis sp. PCC 6803&lt;/i&gt;|, does not appear to be evolutionarily related to the DHNAT enzymes from Arabidopsis. In fact, further analysis reveals that the Arabidopsis enzymes bear more similarity to proteins involved in menaquinone biosynthesis in the eubacterial order |FRAME: TAX-186826 &lt;i&gt;Lactobacillales&lt;/i&gt;|. Therefore, while the Arabidopsis enzymes appear to be part of the TE11/4HBT-II-type subfamily of CoA-thioesterases, their cyanobacterial counterparts belong to their own TE12/Slr0204-type subfamily |CITS:[ 22372525] [15307895]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G48950-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G48950.1" />
